      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as true. The surrounding claim that her love exceeds her powers of speech reinforces the avowal rather than qualifying its truth-conditional form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression affirms and invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond: she positions her love as governed by that bond and subsequently identifies obedience, love, and honour as duties she returns to her father. This is not a new promise with future terms, but the focal avowal itself—through the immediate modifier “According to my bond”—substantially affirms that she stands bound by filial obligations.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a measured answer explaining the proper extent of Cordelia's filial love, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry. Her difficulty verbalizing her heart indicates emotion, but the resulting utterance remains a deliberate avowal bounded by duty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowal and affirmation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled, restrictive answer to Lear's competitive demand: Cordelia refuses to inflate or monopolize love as her sisters have done. “No more nor less” makes this corrective limitation a meaningful residual force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Why have my sisters husbands if they say\\nThey love you all?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while allocating portions of his kingdom. Cordelia answers after refusing to produce the expansive declaration he expects, avowing filial love while defining it by her proper bond and duties rather than by competitive hyperbole.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking—love, obedience, care, and honour owed to her father—rather than creating a new promise. Its extent is expressly limited to what is fitting under that bond and allows for future duties to a husband. Lear does not accept this measured formulation as an adequate answer; he asks her to amend it and warns that it may harm her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports avowal, filial-bond affirmation, and present affection, but it does not independently establish every practical term of Cordelia's obligations or whether Lear ultimately recognises her formulation as valid in this immediate exchange.\"}"
        }
      ],
